  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nCooler, post-cold frontal air remains in place today and \nthrough tonight as high pressure nudges in from the west. High\ntemperatures will struggle to make it into the lower 60s across\nthe lowlands with 50s locking in elsewhere and some 40s across\nthe higher terrain. Some breaks in the stratus deck will occur\nthis afternoon, but the clearing comes in earnest tonight.\nThere are chances for some upslope showers across the northern\nlowlands and mountains this afternoon; it may be cold enough \nlater this afternoon and evening that some ice pellets or \nflurries mix in above 4,000 feet.\n\nLows will drop into the upper 30s and lower 40s tonight. There \nis a possibility that some patchy frost may occur across the \nmountain valleys of Randolph, Pocahontas, and Webster counties \nif clouds clear out tonight and winds decouple. Current thinking\nand guidance suggest that clearing will occur, but pressure \ngradient with arriving high pressure will keep winds breezy \nacross the ridges of the higher elevations. This will primarily \nkeep frost confined to the sheltered mountain valleys that \ndecouple later tonight. That said, opted for a long-fuse \nstatement versus a frost advisory due to expected patchy instead\nof widespread coverage of frost. If low clouds and winds remain\nacross the mountains then frost may not occur at all. \n\nTemperatures will become warmer tomorrow and Saturday with a\nwarm front. 80s are on the board for many lowland locations on\nSaturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nShowers and thunderstorms return to the forecast Saturday with a\nwarm front and cold front approaching from the west. Models show\nan early morning round of showers and isolated thunderstorms \nwith a secondary late afternoon into evening push that will \nshowcase the stronger to severe storms. Warmer temperatures \nwill provide more instability for storms to work with than the \nprevious days.\n\nThe Storm Prediction Center has placed a Marginal Risk (level\n1/5) of severe weather across our Ohio, Kentucky, and western\nWest Virginia counties along the Ohio River for Saturday \nafternoon and evening. Main risks will be damaging winds and \nsmall hail. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA Bermuda High will build off the eastern CONUS later this\nweekend into next week creating a period of heat comparable to\nJuly and August standards. High temperatures Sunday through\nTuesday could reach the upper 80s to low 90s for the lowlands,\nwith many locations in the mountains jumping up into the upper\n70s and 80s. There is a chance that high temperature records \ncould be tied or broken on Monday and Tuesday, especially at\nCharleston, Huntington, Clarksburg, and Parkersburg.\n\nThe blend of models has been overdone with Monday and Tuesday's\nforecasted high temperatures lately; it usually is with our \nfirst heat event of the year. However, it predicts that\ntemperatures across portions of the lowlands will reach the\nupper 90s to near 100 degrees. Nearly every other model is\ntrending to keep temperatures in the upper 80s to lower 90s\nleaving the blend of models the sole outlier. \n\nNo doubt a significant warm up is expected early next week with\nthe building Bermuda High, but a pressure gradient overhead and\nfully bloomed vegetation will likely keep the temperatures from\nrising too far into the 90s.\n\n*SEE THE CLIMATE SECTION FOR FORECAST HIGHS AND PREVIOUS DAILY \n RECORDS*",
